WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.835
What happens when ice 
bears start losing&nbsp;their ice?

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.842
They get stuck on land and they get hungry.

00:00:04.842 --> 00:00:06.972
When polar bears can't 
find good&nbsp;food to eat,

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:08.078
they'll fill up on junk food.

00:00:08.078 --> 00:00:10.711
And for polar bears, junk&nbsp;
food is human food.

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:13.026
This is a rising safety concern for humans.

00:00:13.026 --> 00:00:16.605
After working and living&nbsp;off and on 
with polar bears for over 12 years,

00:00:16.605 --> 00:00:19.025
I know coexisting with them
 can be a challenge,

00:00:19.025 --> 00:00:20.887
and it will only get more challenging

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:24.478
as climate change forces our 
species to increasingly overlap.

00:00:24.479 --> 00:00:27.722
Polar Bears International is 
working on innovative technologies

00:00:27.722 --> 00:00:29.668
that&nbsp;could help provide longer lead times

00:00:29.668 --> 00:00:31.591
between when polar bears and people meet

00:00:31.591 --> 00:00:33.595
or prevent&nbsp;them from meeting altogether.

00:00:33.595 --> 00:00:34.847
Just one example,

00:00:34.847 --> 00:00:35.832
GPS tracking.

00:00:35.832 --> 00:00:38.659
It can tell us where, when and 
why&nbsp;polar bears move.

00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:39.895
It's critical data.

00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:43.621
The number one most important 
coexistence tool we have

00:00:43.621 --> 00:00:46.248
is&nbsp;our willingness to cut carbon emissions

00:00:46.248 --> 00:00:49.538
and stop trapping so much 
heat in our atmosphere.

00:00:49.538 --> 00:00:51.509
The faster we switch to cleaner energies,

00:00:51.509 --> 00:00:55.930
the better we can protect future&nbsp;
generations of polar bears and people.

